/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Chemical energy enters the engine at one rate and leaves at several.
   - Pf is the fuel power in kW, mf is the fuel mass flow in kg/h, CV is the calorific value of the fuel in MJ/kg, and 3.6 converts kg/h × MJ/kg into kW.
   - On the output side there are four streams: brake power Pb at the shaft, heat to the cooling water Pc, heat carried away by the exhaust gas Pe, and the rest Pu, which covers radiation, convection, heat to the lubricating oil (when an oil coo
   - Pf = Pb + Pc + Pe + Pu
   - Pf is fuel power, Pb is brake power, Pc is heat to cooling water, Pe is heat in the exhaust gas, and Pu is the unaccounted remainder, all in kW.
   - Only a few universities teach the heat balance as a full topic, but test cells draw one for every new engine.
   - Brake power comes from the dynamometer (see the chapter on measuring indicated, brake and friction power.
   - Pc = mw × cpw × ΔTw
   - mw is the cooling water mass flow in kg/s, cpw is the specific heat of water, 4.18 kJ/kgK, and ΔTw is the rise in water temperature across the jacket in K.
   - The exhaust term is the sensible heat in the gas above ambient:
*/
