/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Speed changes three things at once: the trapped air mass per cycle, friction and pumping loss.
   - Brake specific fuel consumption (BSFC) is fuel mass flow divided by brake power.
   - Load has a similar curve.
   - ṁf is fuel mass flow in kg/h and BP is brake power in kW, so BSFC is in kg/kWh.
   - Mixture strength is best described by the equivalence ratio φ, the actual fuel-air ratio over the stoichiometric fuel-air ratio: φ = (F/A)/(F/A)stoich = (A/F)stoich/(A/F). φ = 1 is stoichiometric, φ > 1 is rich and φ < 1 is lean.
   - For SI engines, maximum brake power comes from a slightly rich mixture, φ about 1.1: all the oxygen of the trapped air is used and the flame is fast.
   - Spark timing or injection timing sets where combustion occurs relative to top dead centre.
   - Intake air density sets the trapped air mass.
   - Coolant and oil temperature matter.
   - Fuel quality matters.
*/
