/* book Worked example: Q: A naturally aspirated diesel engine gives 40 kW brake power at 25 °C and 1.00 bar on a standard day. It is retested on a hot afternoon at 40 °C and 0.90 bar. Assume brake power follows intake air density. Find the density ratio, the observed brake power and the percentage drop.
   1. Convert the temperatures to kelvin: Tref = 25 + 273 = 298 K and Ttest = 40 + 273 = 313 K.
   2. Use the density ratio: (ptest / pref) × (Tref / Ttest) = (0.90 / 1.00) × (298 / 313) = 0.8569.
   3. Multiply by the reference brake power: Pobs = 40 × 0.8569 = 34.27 kW.
   4. Find the drop: (40 − 34.27) / 40 × 100 = 14.31 %.
   Answer: Density ratio = 0.857, observed brake power = 34.27 kW, a drop of 14.31 %. */
